; Every node's span, read back out of the source it points at.
;
; `span_dump` prints one line per node in `test-parser/main.zn`: the node's kind
; and the text between its span's two positions. That output is checked in as
; `main.spans`, and this rule fails when the two differ.
;
; What it is for: a span is only ever wrong by pointing somewhere, and the rest
; of the suite cannot see where. The acceptance tests ask whether a file parses,
; and the grouping tests ask which shape it parsed to -- neither reads a
; position. So a grammar change that widens a span to swallow a binder, or
; narrows one past its own bracket, passes everything and is noticed only in
; review, if at all.
;
; A failing diff is not by itself a bug: a change to the grammar or to the
; fixture is expected to move spans. Read the diff, satisfy yourself that each
; moved span still covers what its node stands for, and promote it with
; `dune promote`.
; Two fixtures. `main.zn` is the one the parser suite already reaches for, and
; it covers the breadth of the grammar. `utf8.zn` covers the one input class it
; does not: spans are byte offsets, and while a file is ASCII a code-point
; offset is indistinguishable from a byte one. They diverge at the first
; multibyte character and every span after it shifts -- which is exactly the bug
; found on #86, after the suite had been green through it.
(rule
 (with-stdout-to
  main.spans.actual
  (run %{exe:../tools/span_dump.exe} %{dep:../test-parser/main.zn})))

(rule
 (alias runtest)
 (action
  (diff main.spans main.spans.actual)))

(rule
 (with-stdout-to
  utf8.spans.actual
  (run %{exe:../tools/span_dump.exe} %{dep:../test-parser/utf8.zn})))

(rule
 (alias runtest)
 (action
  (diff utf8.spans utf8.spans.actual)))

; The same two fixtures through the desugaring, checked the same way.
;
; `sst_dump` prints each node's variant as well as its kind, so these
; expectations carry two claims at once: that every span still lands on real
; source after nodes have been synthesized, and that each rewrite in
; docs/desugaring.md still produces the shape it says. A `>=` that stopped
; becoming `~(a < b)` is a diff here and nothing anywhere else.
;
; Read a failing diff the same way as the ones above -- a change to the
; grammar, the fixture or the lowering is expected to move these -- then
; promote it with `dune promote`.
(rule
 (with-stdout-to
  main.sst.actual
  (run %{exe:../tools/sst_dump.exe} %{dep:../test-parser/main.zn})))

(rule
 (alias runtest)
 (action
  (diff main.sst main.sst.actual)))

(rule
 (with-stdout-to
  utf8.sst.actual
  (run %{exe:../tools/sst_dump.exe} %{dep:../test-parser/utf8.zn})))

(rule
 (alias runtest)
 (action
  (diff utf8.sst utf8.sst.actual)))

; The desugaring fixture. Unlike the two above it was written for this test:
; every rewrite in docs/desugaring.md 2 appears in it at least once, which the
; other two do not manage -- main.zn reaches only two of the five derived
; operators, so three of them had no expectation before this file.
;
; It gets both dumps. The CST one is what says a rewrite's input was parsed the
; way the fixture means it, which a diff in the SST one alone could not
; distinguish from a lowering change.
(rule
 (with-stdout-to
  desugar.spans.actual
  (run %{exe:../tools/span_dump.exe} %{dep:../test-parser/desugar.zn})))

(rule
 (alias runtest)
 (action
  (diff desugar.spans desugar.spans.actual)))

(rule
 (with-stdout-to
  desugar.sst.actual
  (run %{exe:../tools/sst_dump.exe} %{dep:../test-parser/desugar.zn})))

(rule
 (alias runtest)
 (action
  (diff desugar.sst desugar.sst.actual)))
